# CE Points — versioning & TestFlight builds

## Two different numbers

| Field | Where | Role |
|-------|--------|------|
| **Marketing version** (`expo.version` / `package.json` version, e.g. `1.3.3`) | What users see; App Store Connect “version” | One train of TestFlight builds; one App Store release |
| **Build number** (EAS remote `ios.buildNumber`, e.g. `51`) | Internal; must always increase | Distinguishes each binary uploaded to Apple |

`eas.json` production already has `"autoIncrement": true` and `"appVersionSource": "remote"` — **build number bumps itself** on every EAS iOS production build. Do not manually fight that.

## How to avoid weird App Store version jumps (1.0 → 1.3 with gaps)

Gaps appear when every TestFlight experiment bumps **marketing version**, then only some versions ever get **Released** on the App Store. End users only see Released versions; Connect still lists every created version.

**Default for “build for TestFlight” / iterate polish:**

1. **Keep the same marketing version** (`1.3.3`).
2. Let EAS **autoIncrement the build number** only (`51` → `52` → …).
3. Do **not** bump `app.config.js` / `package.json` version unless one of the cases below.

**Bump marketing version (patch `1.3.3` → `1.3.4`, or minor/major when the change warrants it) when:**

- Starting a **new release train** after the previous version was submitted for App Store review or Released.
- Apple/ASC rejected or blocked uploads for that version string and a new version is required.
- User explicitly asks for a new version / App Store release version.

When bumping: update both `app.config.js` `expo.version` and `package.json` `version` to match. Do not set a local `ios.buildNumber` in app config (remote source owns it).

## Before any EAS production / TestFlight build

1. Confirm whether this is **same-train TestFlight** (default: no marketing bump) or a **new version train** (bump first).
2. Run production iOS build with auto-submit when the user wants TestFlight.
3. Tell the user the resulting **version + build number** and that Apple needs a few minutes to process.

## Do not

- Bump marketing version on every TestFlight build “just in case” — that creates the Connect clutter.
- Confuse marketing version with build number when diagnosing ASC errors (duplicate **build** vs duplicate/invalid **version** are different).
